      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">56 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and Caesar renew their alliance at Ravenna and Luca, and Crassus arranges to have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> as his province</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">54 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> invades the Parthian client-state of Osroene while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surena">Surena</a> defeats and executes Mithridates IV at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">53 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">In the spring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> rejects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>'s offer and marches through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">53 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surena">Surena</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cataphract">cataphracts</a> defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> near Carrhae, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-licinius-crassus">Publius</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> dies with his force</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">53 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> is killed at a truce meeting, and Cassius leads about 10,000 survivors back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">40 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A Parthian invasion is stopped and repulsed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-licinius-crassus">Publius</a> Ventidius Bassus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">36 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> in the Atropatene campaign, which also fails</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-carrhae" data-v="bce7a41b6d" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Romans form a hollow square</b> Crassus first agreed to a line with infantry in the centre and cavalry on the wings, then redeployed his men into a hollow square. The Parthian cataphracts advanced, their armour covered in cloths.</li><li><b>Horse archers surround the square</b> Surena sent his horse archers round the Roman square, beyond the range of the pila. Crassus sent his skirmishers out to drive them off, and the archers' arrows drove the skirmishers back.</li><li><b>The cataphracts charge the testudo</b> The legionaries locked their shields in the testudo, which limited their fighting in melee. The cataphracts repeatedly charged the line, and when the Romans loosened their formation the cataphracts drew back and the horse archers shot again.</li><li><b>Publius is drawn away</b> Crassus sent his son Publius with Gallic cavalry, archers and eight cohorts to drive off the horse archers. They feigned retreat and drew Publius away from the main army, shooting at his force as it followed.</li><li><b>Publius's force is cut off on a hill</b> Once Publius was well apart from the army, the cataphracts confronted his force and the horse archers cut off its retreat. The Gauls retreated to a hill, where Publius and his officers killed themselves. Only 500 Gauls were taken alive.</li><li><b>The Romans advance and are attacked until nightfall</b> Crassus ordered a general advance and was shown his son's head on a spear. The horse archers shot at the Romans from all directions and the cataphracts charged in a series of attacks that went on until nightfall.</li><li><b>The retreat towards Carrhae</b> Crassus ordered a disorganised retreat towards Carrhae, leaving 4,000 wounded behind. Four cohorts lost their way in the dark and were surrounded on a hill, where only 20 Romans survived.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_(26724093101)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The war grew out of the arrangements of the First Triumvirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a>, Pompeius Magnus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. At meetings at Ravenna and Luca in March and April 56 BCE the three agreed to secure legislation prolonging Caesar's Gallic command and to put Crassus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> forward for a second joint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>. The faction won the consulship, and the Lex Trebonia gave the two outgoing consuls proconsulships of five years. Pompey received the Spanish provinces, and Crassus arranged to have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with the transparent intention of going to war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>.</p><p>In Parthia a war of succession had broken out in 57 BCE, after King Phraates III was killed by his sons Orodes II and Mithridates IV. Orodes forced Mithridates to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-gabinius">Aulus Gabinius</a>, the Roman proconsul of Syria, who abandoned plans to use him and intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> instead. Gabinius's successor Crassus also sought to ally with Mithridates and invaded the Parthian client-state of Osroene in 54 BCE, but spent most of his time waiting for reinforcements while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surena">Surena</a> besieged, defeated and executed Mithridates at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>.</p><p>Crassus was about 62 and very wealthy. Ancient sources, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, name greed as his motive. Erich S. Gruen believes he meant to enrich the public treasury, while most modern historians point to greed, envy of Pompey's exploits and rivalry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> called the war nulla causa, as Parthia had a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the tribune Ateius Capito performed a public ritual of execration as Crassus prepared to leave. Crassus left Rome on 14 November 55 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> raised his army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with his own wealth. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> he assembled seven legions, about 28,000 to 35,000 heavy infantry, with about 4,000 light infantry and 4,000 cavalry, including 1,000 Gallic horse brought by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-licinius-crassus">Publius</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artavasdes-ii-of-armenia">Artavasdes II of Armenia</a> advised him to go through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> to avoid the desert and offered a further 10,000 cavalry and 30,000 infantry. Crassus refused twice, the second time when Artavasdes came to his camp with 6,000 cavalry, and chose the direct route through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</p><p>Orodes divided the Parthian army. He took most of it, mainly foot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> with a small amount of cavalry, to punish the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, and sent an all-cavalry force under the spahbod <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surena">Surena</a> to scout and harass Crassus. Plutarch describes it as a thousand mail-clad horsemen and a still greater number of light-armed cavalry. With slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> Surena's expedition numbered ten thousand, supported by a baggage train of 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>. Orodes did not expect it to defeat Crassus and wanted only to delay him.</p><p>Crassus took directions from the Osroene chieftain Ariamnes, who was in Parthian pay and urged him to attack at once, saying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> were weak. Plutarch says Ariamnes led the army into the most desolate part of the desert, far from water. Gregory Hospodor has found that Crassus followed a well-travelled caravan route past several towns on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikh-river">Balikh River</a>, with ample water from spring runoff, which casts doubt on the claim of sabotage. On 9 June the scouts were found slain and a large force of cavalry was approaching.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On hearing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> the Roman army panicked. Cassius recommended the traditional order, infantry in the centre and cavalry on the wings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> first agreed but then formed a hollow square, each side of twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a>, which guarded against outflanking at the cost of mobility. At a stream his generals advised camping, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-licinius-crassus">Publius</a> persuaded Crassus to confront the Parthians at once.</p><p>The Parthians beat hollow drums, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surena">Surena</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cataphract">cataphracts</a> dropped cloths from their armour to reveal it. Surena judged a charge would not yet break the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and sent his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a> round the square, beyond the range of the pila. His skirmishers were driven back. When the Romans advanced, the horse archers retreated and shot as they withdrew. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a> formed the testudo, which restricted their melee fighting, and the cataphracts charged repeatedly, causing panic and heavy casualties. When the Romans loosened their ranks, the cataphracts withdrew and the horse archers shot at the exposed men.</p><p>Crassus hoped the arrows would run out, but Surena resupplied them with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>. Crassus sent Publius with 1,300 Gallic cavalry, 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and eight cohorts against the horse archers. They <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a> and drew him off, the cataphracts then confronted his force and the horse archers cut off its retreat. Publius and his officers threw themselves on their swords, and only 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> were taken alive. Crassus was shown his son's head on a spear. The attacks went on until nightfall, and a ragged retreat to Carrhae left 4,000 wounded, who were killed next morning. Four cohorts lost in the dark were surrounded on a hill, and only 20 Romans survived.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/euripides"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euripides.jpg" alt="Euripides" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Euripide_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NikonZ7II, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The next day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surena">Surena</a> offered to negotiate and proposed a truce allowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in exchange for all territory east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> was reluctant to meet, but his troops threatened to mutiny. At the meeting a Parthian pulled at Crassus's reins and violence broke out in which Crassus and his generals were killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> allegedly poured molten gold down his throat, mocking his greed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> reports that his severed head was used in a performance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a>' Bacchae at a banquet before the king. The remaining Romans tried to flee, but most were captured or killed. Plutarch gives about 20,000 killed and 10,000 captured, and says Parthian casualties were minimal.</p><p>The Parthians captured several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> eagles. Plutarch also mentions that they dressed the prisoner who most resembled Crassus as a woman and paraded him through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, ordering the other prisoners to hail him as Imperator, in mockery of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-triumph">Roman triumph</a>. The 10,000 prisoners appear to have been deported to Alexandria <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>) near the north-eastern border, where they reportedly married local people.</p><p>Orodes II defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and took their country, but Surena's victory made the king jealous, and he had Surena executed. His son Pacorus then led an unsuccessful campaign into Syria. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus">Gaius Cassius Longinus</a>, a quaestor under Crassus, led about 10,000 survivors back to Syria and governed it for two more years as proquaestor. He resisted the Parthian siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and ambushed Pacorus's army at Antigonea as it retreated, killing the Parthian second-in-command Osaces, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> praised him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ovid"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ovid.jpg" alt="Ovid" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ovid_by_an_anonymous_sculptor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucasaw, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The account of the fighting rests largely on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, whose figures for the armies and for the casualties are the ones usually repeated, and who blames <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> for every aspect of the defeat, particularly his tactics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> gives the date of 9 June in Book 6 of the Fasti. Plutarch is also the source of the tales of the molten gold, the performance of the Bacchae and the false Crassus paraded through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>.</p><p>Historians dispute several points. Hospodor questions Plutarch's suggestion that Ariamnes led Crassus into a waterless desert on purpose, and finds Plutarch's account of Crassus's second meeting with Artavasdes questionable. Some historians describe arrows partly penetrating the Roman shields and nailing them to the limbs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a> and their feet to the ground, while Plutarch wrote that arrows passed through every kind of cover, and others state that most wounds were non-fatal hits to exposed limbs. It is also uncertain whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surena">Surena</a> himself or an emissary went to ask for the peace conference. In the 1940s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> H. Dubs proposed that the people of Liqian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> descended from the Roman prisoners; no coins, weapons or other artefacts of a Roman presence have been found at Zhelaizhai, and the vast majority of historians do not accept it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pompey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompey.jpg" alt="Pompey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Pompey_the_Great,_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Didier Descouens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Parthian capture of the golden eagles was considered a grave moral defeat and an evil omen for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. When Caesar was assassinated in 44 BCE he was planning a retaliatory war. The battle was one of the first major battles between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, and it led Parthia to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> several times, with varying success. Rome also realised that its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a> could not fight Parthian cavalry effectively without support in open terrain.</p><p>An old theory holds that the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a>, with that of Julia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s wife and Caesar's daughter, in 54 BCE, severed the ties between Caesar and Pompey and ended the First Triumvirate, so that Caesar's civil war followed. Several historians note the time that passed between Crassus's death and the outbreak of civil war. Stern has claimed that the death nearly cut the links the Triumvirate had with the aristocracy, leaving the state vulnerable to friction that turned into civil war. In a regional context the battle had little negative impact for Rome in the long term: the Parthian invasion of 40 BCE was stopped and repulsed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-licinius-crassus">Publius</a> Ventidius Bassus, and Antony's invasion of Parthia in 36 BCE, though it also ended in failure, was not prevented.</p><p>Hospodor argues that Crassus is to blame tactically only for the poorly organised retreat, and that blaming him alone oversimplifies the result, which exposed both the strengths and the weaknesses of the Roman military system of the late Republic. He suggests Crassus's adherence to doctrine may have led him to trust heavy infantry against cavalry.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Carrhae in 53 BCE a Parthian cavalry force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surena">Surena</a>, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cataphract">cataphracts</a>, defeated the Roman army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a>. Crassus was killed at a truce meeting, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> gives about 20,000 Roman dead and 10,000 prisoners.</em></p>

